receiver allen robinson one of the guys  entering a contract here shireen who  didn't get a long-term deal now  fully and officially disgruntled and  reportedly has asked for a trade out of  town as he gets one game into what is  the final year of that deal your  reaction to alan robinson wanting out  supposedly  well you know ryan pace the general  manager of the bears said last week that  there was no deadline on these deals but  the bears typically as most teams do get  their long term deals with their core  players wrapped up before the season  opener now here we are past the season  opener in week two and he still doesn't  have his deal and it tells you that they  haven't made extensive negotiations  progress on negotiations  and so he has asked for a trade  according to brad biggs of the chicago  tribune and is looking to get out  who knows where this goes at this point  but he scrubbed all mention of the bears  from his social media and is obviously  not happy with what's going on these  things tend to get worked out one way or  the other whether that means a trade or  whether that means a long-term deal it's  probably going to get worked out one of  those ways  i think we can say football season  officially has begun when a player  scrubs all mention of his team from his  social media accounts and every time i  even think that much less writers say it  i realize how trite and superficial that  is but that's how people send messages  nowadays what they allow onto their  social media page what's on their  profile what's in their background what  they are wearing all of that stuff shows  how they feel and if you take all  references to the bears off your social  media page and you're a member of the  bears that may mean that you'll want out  last year 98 catches a career high for  alan robinson  1147 yards and seven touchdowns he's  still yet to live up to  the standard he set in his second year  in jacksonville in 2015 but a lot of  that was garbage time production all due  respect that was the year that we let  ourselves think blake bortles is a good  quarterback  uh they were behind they threw the ball  they gained a lot of yardage that way i  think alan hearns also had a thousand  receiving yards that year in 2015 if not  a thousand close to it but since then  883 2017 he had 17 after he suffered a  week one torn acl 754 his first year in  chicago he's now climbed back to 11 47.  i can see why he wants to get paid and  you know when you see  a guy like marlon mack in indy in a  contract year shireen suffer a torn  achilles tendon and he's done for the  year after one week and when your  personal history in the nfl includes a  week one acl tear you know what the risk  is and you know what can happen and his  market goes goes out the window if he  suffers an injury that ends his season  and sends him on to the free agent  market while he's recovering from some  sort of a season-ending injury so i  understand why he wants his deal  no question about it he's making 10.9  million this year and he looked at what  keenan allen got there have been  obviously a lot of receiver contracts  that we've seen this off season but  keenan allen compares favorably keenan  allen's played one more year than alan  robinson has but if you observe their  stats they're they're pretty darn close  to what they've done in fact alan  robinson has only won fewer touchdowns  than keenan robertson than kenyon allen  does despite paint playing one fewer  years and i would argue that alan  robinson has not played with very many  good quarterbacks if any good  quarterbacks in his career as you said  blake bortles and now he's with mitchell  trubisky so he hasn't played with a  really good quarterback he's only 27.  somebody's gonna want him whether it's  now in a trade whether it's after this  season with a big contract if they can't  get the extension done somebody is going  to want alan robinson is he worth 20  million a year i think that's what the  bears probably are grappling with at  this point is he worth that and i'm not  sure he is but that's what the market  bears  and i think part of the challenge when  you pay a receiver big money is having  the confidence that you will have a  quarterback who can allow you to get the  return on your investment that has to be  part of this they don't know who their  quarterback is going to be next year  their starter mitchell trubisky's in the  last year of his contract why because  they didn't pick up the fifth year  option why because he stunk last year  and to consider that alan robinson got  1147 receiving yards in a year where  mitchell trubisky played like crap i  think we can say crap at 5 p.m eastern  time we definitely can say it at 11 p.m  local time in the uk we have done the  research crap is acceptable then i it  really creates a conundrum for the bears  on what they're going to do because  who's the quarterback going to be and  what kind of production are you going to  get and do you really  get the bang for your buck if you give  this guy big money was last year a fluke  that's the thing he's got two full  seasons with the bears 754 in the year  the team was good 11 47 in the year at a  team was bad what do you really get for  this guy over the long haul  well and the question for the bears is  what's going to happen after this year  as you said with the quarterback but  with the gm with the coach with  everything else this is a a team with a  roster that's very much in flux heading  into next year and a lot depends  obviously on how they do this year  they need a happy and healthy allen  robinson if they're going to do what  they think they can do which is  duplicate a couple years ago when they  got to the playoffs and they were a  really good football team but they need  allen robinson they need him to be happy  if that's going to happen so they're  they're to me they're they're really in  a bad spot with how much to give him  what the future looks 